#include <stdio.h>
#include <ctype.h>

int main(void) {
    long long lines = 0;
    long long words = 0;
    long long chars = 0;
    int in_word = 0;
    int ch;

    while ((ch = getchar()) != EOF) {
        chars++;
        if (ch == '\n') {
            lines++;
        }

        if (isspace(ch)) {
            in_word = 0;
        } else if (!in_word) {
            in_word = 1;
            words++;
        }
    }

    printf("%lld %lld %lld\n", lines, words, chars);
    return 0;
}

description:
In systems programming and embedded databases, fixed-size binary records are stored on disk and accessed using file seek operations (`fseek`, `ftell`, `fwrite`, `fread`). Because records have a uniform byte width, the byte offset for record $i$ is calculated directly as $offset = i \times \text{sizeof}(\text{Record})$, permitting $O(1)$ random access without scanning preceding records.

Simulate a binary record file in memory using the following C structure:
```c
typedef struct {
    int id;
    char name[24];
    int score;
    int is_active;
} Record;
```
The file manager starts empty and processes $Q$ commands sequentially:
1. `INSERT <id> <name> <score>`: Writes a new record at the end of the file with `is_active = 1`.
2. `READ <index>`: Seeks to slot `index` (0-indexed). If `index` is within bounds and the record has `is_active == 1`, print:
   `ID: <id>, Name: <name>, Score: <score>`
   Otherwise, print `RECORD NOT FOUND`.
3. `UPDATE <index> <new_score>`: Seeks to slot `index`. If within bounds and `is_active == 1`, update `score` to `new_score` and print `RECORD UPDATED`. Otherwise, print `UPDATE FAILED`.
4. `DELETE <index>`: Seeks to slot `index`. If within bounds and `is_active == 1`, set `is_active = 0` (soft delete) and print `RECORD DELETED`. Otherwise, print `DELETE FAILED`.

